## Reading the numbers

- **The rule beats the most-visited baseline by {{pt:methods.full.top3-methods.most_visited.top3}} points top-3** and by
  {{pt:methods.full.top1-methods.most_visited.top1}} points top-1.
- **Knowing the time of day is worth {{pt:methods.full.top3-methods.full_no_time.top3}} points top-3.** Without it, the rule is no better than plain Markov.
- **The hard ceiling is {{pct:full_extras.ceiling_top_any}}.** {{pct:full_extras.never_visited_before}} of test check-ins are at a place the
  user had never been before. No method that only ranks the user's own places can get those, so
  even a perfect ranker of known places would top out at {{pct:full_extras.ceiling_top_any}}.
- **More history helps.** Users with 200+ earlier check-ins get {{pct:full_extras.top3_by_history.200+.top3}} top-3, against {{pct:full_extras.top3_by_history.<50.top3}} for users with fewer than 50.
- **Don't quote 90%.** Predicting the exact venue among {{int:dataset.users}} users' tens of thousands of places is
  hard. If a higher headline is wanted, the category ({{pct:full_extras.top3_category}}) and within-1 km
  ({{pct:full_extras.top3_within_1km}}) figures are real, but they answer easier questions and must be labelled as such.

## Comparison with the spec's reference run (§5.2)

Every top-1/3/5 figure is within ±1 point of the reference; most match to 0.1 point. Known differences:

| Item | Reference | Ours | Cause |
|---|---|---|---|
| Test window start | 2012-11-23 20:58 UTC | {{raw:dataset.cutoff_utc}} UTC | We cut at exactly 80% of de-duplicated check-ins (index ⌊0.8·N⌋). The reference's 20:58 isn't any clean 80% index of the raw, de-duplicated or collapsed rows, so its exact rule is unknown |
| Test check-ins | 42,659 | {{int:dataset.test_checkins}} | Follows from the cutoff above (2 check-ins) |
| Popularity-only MRR | 0.015 | {{num3:methods.popularity.mrr}} | Not confirmed. Our baseline ranks every venue in the city (the current one excluded). Top-1/3/5 agree within 0.1 point, so the difference is in the long tail of ranks, which MRR weights and top-k ignores |
| Category accuracy | 47.4% | {{pct:full_extras.top3_category}} | Matches once "top 3 categories" means the 3 categories with the highest summed score. Taking the categories of the top-3 venues instead gives {{pct:full_extras.top3_venue_category}} |

## Reproduce

```bash
python scripts/import_dataset.py --data data/raw/dataset_TSMC2014_NYC.txt
python scripts/evaluate.py --data data/raw/dataset_TSMC2014_NYC.txt
python scripts/render_docs.py
```

Changing a weight in `config.py` or `.env` is a design change: re-run the three commands and
report the new table. Weights were not chosen by looking at these results.
